    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "1 intersection, at $(-2,-2)$.\n\nCompleting the square gives radii $5$ and $1$ and centre distance $d=4=5-1$, so the circles touch internally.",
      "worked": "For $C_1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-6x+y^2+4y-12&=0\\\\(x-3)^2-9+(y+2)^2-4-12&=0\\\\(x-3)^2+(y+2)^2&=25\\end{align*}$$\nIts centre is $(3,-2)$ and its radius is $5$.\n\nFor $C_2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+2x+y^2+4y+4&=0\\\\(x+1)^2-1+(y+2)^2-4+4&=0\\\\(x+1)^2+(y+2)^2&=1\\end{align*}$$\nIts centre is $(-1,-2)$ and its radius is $1$.\n\nThe centres have the same $y$-coordinate, so their distance apart is\n$$\\begin{align*}d&=|3-(-1)|\\\\d&=4.\\end{align*}$$\nFor positive radii and distinct centres, $d<|r_1-r_2|$ means one circle lies inside the other without touching, while $d>r_1+r_2$ means they are separate. If $|r_1-r_2|<d<r_1+r_2$, they intersect twice. Equality at the lower boundary gives internal tangency; equality at the upper boundary gives external tangency.\n\nHere,\n$$\\begin{align*}|r_1-r_2|&=5-1\\\\|r_1-r_2|&=4.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $d=|r_1-r_2|$. The smaller circle lies inside the larger circle and touches it once, at $(-2,-2)$.\n\nThere is **1 intersection**.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
